WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:01.794
Let me give you some advice.

00:00:01.794 --> 00:00:04.019
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:04.019 --> 00:00:07.500
The process of doing
your second draft

00:00:07.500 --> 00:00:10.260
is a process of making
it look like you knew

00:00:10.260 --> 00:00:14.550
what you were doing all along.

00:00:14.550 --> 00:00:17.490
Writing a novel is like
driving through the fog

00:00:17.490 --> 00:00:18.760
with one headlight out.

00:00:18.760 --> 00:00:21.160
You can't see very
far ahead of yourself.

00:00:21.160 --> 00:00:23.480
But every now and again,
the mists will clear.

00:00:27.070 --> 00:00:30.570
In my class, I'm going to
tell you what stories are.

00:00:30.570 --> 00:00:32.470
I'm going to give you some tips.

00:00:32.470 --> 00:00:35.607
I'm going to give you some
exercises, and most of all,

00:00:35.607 --> 00:00:38.190
I'm going to give you a little
push forward that you might not

00:00:38.190 --> 00:00:38.690
have had.

00:00:42.001 --> 00:00:42.501
Here we go.

00:00:42.501 --> 00:00:45.397
I'll go through the whole thing,
because it's fairly short,

00:00:45.397 --> 00:00:47.480
and it does a whole bunch
of stuff with character.

00:00:47.480 --> 00:00:51.030
And then I'll tell
you what I did.

00:00:51.030 --> 00:00:52.360
I have writer's block.

00:00:52.360 --> 00:00:53.400
I cannot write.

00:00:53.400 --> 00:00:56.640
Sounds like something that
you can do nothing about.

00:00:56.640 --> 00:00:58.710
That, of course, isn't true.

00:00:58.710 --> 00:01:03.380
Why don't I take you through
the process of plotting a comic?

00:01:03.380 --> 00:01:04.920
Sandman 19.

00:01:04.920 --> 00:01:07.690
I can give you how I
do it and how I did it.

00:01:07.690 --> 00:01:09.690
You do it like this,
your thumbnails

00:01:09.690 --> 00:01:10.987
will only be seen by you.

00:01:10.987 --> 00:01:12.570
You definitely don't
have to show them

00:01:12.570 --> 00:01:14.300
to an artist who
can actually draw.

00:01:17.018 --> 00:01:21.140
Worldbuilding is honestly the
joy of getting to play God.

00:01:21.140 --> 00:01:23.870
Every little detail that
you can smuggle with you

00:01:23.870 --> 00:01:26.480
into your fiction
makes your world

00:01:26.480 --> 00:01:28.240
more real for your reader.

00:01:28.240 --> 00:01:30.906
And you know, of course, that they're
just smudges in the background.

00:01:30.906 --> 00:01:32.690
But that's OK, because
it was informed

00:01:32.690 --> 00:01:35.369
by you and an imagination.

00:01:35.369 --> 00:01:38.770
Human beings are
storytelling creatures.

00:01:38.770 --> 00:01:40.990
Stories are vital.

00:01:40.990 --> 00:01:44.930
We convey truth with stories.

00:01:44.930 --> 00:01:48.050
That is the magic of fiction.

00:01:48.050 --> 00:01:51.270
You need that conviction
that you are brilliant.

00:01:51.270 --> 00:01:53.600
This is the greatest idea
that anybody's ever had.

00:01:53.600 --> 00:01:58.355
And that by writing it, you
will set the world on fire.

00:01:58.355 --> 00:02:03.550
I'm Neil Gaiman, and
this is my MasterClass.

